<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  examples/fixed-vault/AndroidManifest.xml

  The remediated half of chapter 33's example pair. Diff this against
  examples/leaky-vault/AndroidManifest.xml; the differences are small, and only
  two of the five real fixes are visible here at all. That asymmetry is the
  lesson: a manifest audit finds the manifest bugs, and the manifest bugs are
  the minority.

  VISIBLE HERE

    android:allowBackup="false"
        The blunt fix, and the correct one for an app whose entire purpose is
        holding a credential. It removes the app from Auto Backup and from
        adb backup outright.

        If the app DOES need backup — most do, because users expect their data
        to follow them to a new phone — then keep allowBackup="true" and
        exclude the sensitive files instead. backup_rules.xml in this directory
        is a working example, wired in with:

            android:fullBackupContent="@xml/backup_rules"          (API 23-30)
            android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"  (API 31+)

        Ship both attributes. The platform reads the one that matches its API
        level and ignores the other, and an app that sets only the newer one is
        unprotected on every device below API 31. See examples/README.md.

    android:debuggable removed
        The leaky app sets it. A debuggable app lets `run-as` read the entire
        credential-encrypted directory with no root at all, which collapses
        every "only root can reach this" argument in the whole chapter. It is
        also the flag that lets any local process attach a debugger and read
        the decrypted secret out of memory.

  NOT VISIBLE HERE, AND THIS IS THE POINT

    The plaintext staging file, the debug export on external storage, the
    duplicate token in SharedPreferences, the Log.d, the write to
    device-encrypted storage, the WAL residue, and FLAG_SECURE are ALL fixed in
    the Java, and NONE of them is expressible in a manifest. A scanner that
    reads only AndroidManifest.xml grades the leaky app and this one almost
    identically.

  AUTHORISED TESTING ONLY — this app still stores a demo secret. It is a
  teaching artefact, not a product.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    package="com.pentest.ch33.vaultfixed">

    <application
        android:label="CH33 Fixed Vault"
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:theme="@android:style/Theme.Material.Light">

        <!-- singleTop for the same testing reason as the leaky app: without it,
             a second `am start` for an already-top activity never reaches
             onNewIntent, and a scripted multi-step exercise silently runs only
             its first step. See examples/leaky-vault/AndroidManifest.xml. -->
        <activity
            android:name=".VaultActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:launchMode="singleTop"
            android:label="CH33 Fixed Vault">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>
    </application>
</manifest>
